"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 9.5 and an ISUP grade group 3 finding on the prostatectomy specimen. Positive prognostic indicators include negative surgical margins, no seminal vesicle invasion, and absence of lymphovascular invasion. Countervailing findings are limited; specifically, no lymph nodes were removed, meaning nodal status is unknown, which widens uncertainty. The biopsy history shows a progression from Gleason 0+0 to Gleason 3+4, suggesting potential underlying aggressiveness not fully captured by the initial sampling.\n\nPrognostic revision would be significantly aided by postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory. A rising or persistently elevated PSA warrants re-evaluation of risk stratification. Given the lack of nodal assessment, this remains a key area of uncertainty impacting definitive staging. The current data are insufficient to provide a more refined prognosis without further biochemical monitoring. The fixed estimated horizon is 69.39 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 45.11–93.68 months; its coverage has not been validated."
